Durarara!! AU
Chapter 23 – Part 2
Celty sat on the edge of the spare bed and did not lie down.
The room was fine. Sheets made. Door mostly open. No stains. No Shinra voice performing care from a kitchen. No Mei. It should have been the safer version of the night — the one she had chosen on purpose so she would not turn a soft landing into a second life.
It was also empty in a way the dark of two nights ago had not been.
The thought arrived before she could stop it.
It would be easier to sleep beside him again.
Not easier politically. Not easier with the house across the ward still wiped clean. Easier in the body. Easier in the place where a person without a head still felt the absence of another presence like weather. Last time she had pulled him under the covers and pretended the comparison to Shinra was only size and heat. Last time she had woken to a boy who could hear her and did not flinch from the smoke. The spare room had none of that. It had principle. Principle did not warm the mattress.
Black smoke curled once in the dark, irritated with her.
You told him not to become whatever you need.
You chose this room so you would mean it.
If you walk back out there and ask for the bed, you are not asking for safety. You are asking for the thing you said you would not steal while your own house is still lying.
She knew that.
She also knew the other truth sitting under it: sleeping alone after photographing a desk and hearing a month ago was going to keep her staring at the ceiling until morning. Beside him, the noise in her chest had somewhere to go. Beside him, the clean furniture across town felt one room farther away.
Celty did not stand up yet.
She stayed on the edge of the spare bed, helmetless, argument running in a circle with no face to show it, and let the harder sentence finish itself.
Easier is not the same as right.
But easier is still true.
And he already said the door stays open either direction.
Outside the mostly-shut door, the flat was quiet in the way of music sealed inside someone else’s headphones. Curtis on the couch. Her in the room she had chosen. The night offering a simpler geometry if she only crossed the hallway and asked.
She did not cross it.
Not yet.
She only sat with the thought, and hated how much sense it made.
With the music still sealed in the black AirPods Max, Curtis opened his phone and went into the cloud.
The suite stayed quiet around him. Celty was on the other side of a mostly-shut door. The kit was closed. The USB was still on the table, unplugged. This was smaller than plugging the whole archive into the television — just the phone, the scroll, the private inventory of a life that had ended a month ago and still lived in a camera roll.
He swiped.
Carlos and Maria first, caught mid-laugh in some parking lot glow, already leaning into each other like the argument of how they met had been settled on contact. Curtis’s mouth tugged under the leftover evening heat.
“Heh. Carlos, you and Maria were such lovebirds the moment you both met.”
Another swipe.
Adolf and Leona — a frame from before the truce, posture all knives, the kind of photo that only became funny after the fact. Curtis huffed a real quiet laugh into the empty living room.
“Oh. I almost forgot this. When Adolf met Leona.” A second under his breath. “Hahahaha. Those two wanted to kill each other. No one would ever guess a German named Adolf would end up dating a Jewish girl.”
The next photo stopped the scroll.
Him and Justice. Seventeen. Some bar in L.A., still sweat-bright from a stage, smiling like the night had finally paid them for surviving everything before it. Arms around each other’s shoulders. The kind of picture that made the month feel surgically small.
“…I remember this. It was right after we won a band talent show. Leona took it.”
He looked at it longer than the others. Long black braids fell forward as he tipped his head, mismatched eyes reading a version of his own face that still had a brother attached to it. The music kept going in the headphones. The flat did not. For a second the spare-room door and the erased desk and the soft landing all sat outside the frame, and there was only L.A., a win, and Justice alive enough to lean into the shot.
Curtis locked the phone.
He set it aside on the couch cushion, face down, as if the cloud could be put to bed the same way a person could.
The AirPods Max stayed on.
The photo stayed behind the lock.
And the boy who had been seventeen in a bar in L.A. sat in a furnished Ikebukuro flat a month after the end of that smile, listening to music so the kitchen would not have to hear him say anything else out loud.
Meanwhile, Celty stepped out of the room.
The argument with herself had not ended in a clean decision. Only in movement. Black smoke trailed low behind her as she crossed the short hallway, spare-room principle still intact in theory and already losing to the simpler fact that the dark had been too empty after a desk that cleaned itself and a boy who had said a month ago like the number still had teeth.
She stopped at the edge of the living room.
Curtis was on the couch.
Red cap off. Black shirt still on. Long braids loose. Black AirPods Max sealed over his ears, eyes closed, phone face-down on the cushion beside him like he had just put a whole other country to sleep. The kit sat untouched on the table. The USB too. He looked less like a host and less like a soft landing and more like a person who had finally run out of rooms to perform in, so he had put music where the silence would have hurt.
Celty stood there and did not announce herself at once.
The private channel could have cut straight through the headphones. She knew that. She had used it on him in louder places than this. For a second she only watched the rise of his chest, the stillness of someone listening hard enough to keep the past from climbing out of a locked phone.
He looks like he went somewhere again. Not the explosion. The other place. The one with the pictures.
She took one more step into the room, bare presence in the low TV light, and let the link open softer than a door knock.
Curtis.
If the music was too loud, the name would still reach him.
If it did not, she would wait in the doorway of a choice she had already half-made by leaving the spare bed empty behind her.
Curtis paused the music and pulled the headphones down around his neck, black AirPods Max resting against the loose braids. Mismatched eyes opened on Celty already in the living room, not in the spare-room doorway where he had left the night arranged.
“Ya?”
Not sharp. Just present. The phone stayed face-down. The USB stayed unplugged. He looked at her like a person who had been deep in a camera roll and was now being asked to re-enter the current tense.
Celty held still for a second under that attention.
The private channel came through quieter than the demand about the stains, quieter than don’t become whatever.
I couldn’t sleep in there.
A short pause. Black smoke ticked once above her collar, almost embarrassed by the simplicity of it.
“I keep choosing the principled room and then staring at the ceiling.” She did not move closer yet. “Two days ago I stayed beside you and told myself it was curiosity. Tonight I told myself the spare bed was the correct distance. Both versions are still awake.”
Her gloved hands flexed at her sides.
“I am not asking you to be whatever I need. I already said that.” A thinner beat. “I am asking if the couch still has space, or if the offer to stay by you expired when I picked the other door first.”
She stood in his living room with the headphones around his neck and the locked phone full of a life a month gone, and let the true smaller request sit where the big ones had been.
“I do not want to be alone with the clean desk in my head. That is all. If the answer is no, I will go back to the spare room and mean it this time.”
Curtis did not make her hold the question in the air.
“Why would I say no to you, Celty.” It was not really a question. He shifted on the couch, black AirPods Max still around his neck, and tipped his head toward the empty stretch of cushion. “Please join me. I’m just… relaxing, in a sense.”
The in a sense carried the month, the photos, the kit, and the fact that relaxation for him was often just noise loud enough to keep the past from standing up. He did not dress it in more than that.
Celty came the rest of the way.
She sat beside him, not on top of the distance she had tried to preserve in the spare room, and not climbing into the shape of two nights ago either. Just close enough that the clean desk across the ward had somewhere else to sit. Black smoke settled low. The private channel stayed open without demanding anything.
Thank you.
Then, quieter, almost dry:
Relaxing. You say that like a man who just locked a whole life inside a phone and called it peace.
She did not reach for the USB. She did not ask to see the pictures. She only let her shoulder find a careful line near his arm, the helmet still on the table, the spare room left empty on purpose this time for a different reason.
“I will take the couch,” she said. “Not the speech. Not the whatever. Just this.”
Curtis left the headphones down. The music stayed paused. For a moment the furnished flat was only two people on a couch that had not been chosen by either of them, one trying to relax in a sense, the other refusing to stare at a ceiling alone, and neither of them pretending the night was simple.
Celty pulled in close.
Not a climb into his lap. Not a return to the covers of two nights ago. Just a shift along the couch until their legs were touching, solid and ordinary, the kind of contact that said I am here without asking the night to become a claim. Black smoke held still. The private channel stayed quiet enough to count as rest.
Curtis’s expression softened.
The hard line from a month ago, from the locked phone, from the erased desk, eased under it without turning into appetite or performance. He did not put an arm around her. He did not tip the moment into a test of the soft landing. He let the place where their legs met be enough, and reached instead for a book on the desk — something already half-read, pages broken in, the civilian object in a room that had spent the evening on kits and photographs.
He settled back with it open against his knee, long black braids falling forward as he found his place, AirPods Max still around his neck like a tool he no longer needed now that the silence had company.
Celty watched the choice for a second.
He could have asked. He could have closed the distance the rest of the way. He reached for paper.
The private thought arrived warmer than she wanted to admit.
He is not taking. He is staying. That is different.
She left their legs aligned and let the book become the third presence on the couch: a headless woman, a boy who would not force the night, and a story that had nothing to do with stains or brothers or final stops, turning quietly under the low light while Ikebukuro went on without them.
Celty watched him turn a page before she spoke.
The private channel came through low, almost careful with the quiet he had built around the book and the place where their legs touched.
You did not reach for me.
A short pause. Not accusation. Recognition.
“Most people would have. After I left the spare room. After I sat this close.” Black smoke ticked once, then settled. “Shinra would have turned it into a speech about destiny and the shape of my soul. You reached for a book.”
She looked at the open pages against his knee, then at his face in the low light.
“That is why this works better than it should.” Her voice stayed soft. “I can sit here and not be a crisis you have to solve with your hands. I can be tired. You can be somewhere in a story that is not mine. Our legs can touch and it does not have to become a decision about the rest of the night.”
A thinner beat, almost reluctant to admit it.
“I like this version of the landing. The one that does not demand I become a guest in your bed to prove I stayed.”
She let her shoulder settle a fraction nearer his arm, still not climbing, still not taking more than the contact she had already claimed.
“Read. I will be here. That is enough sentence for me right now.”
Celty did not stop.
The quiet permission of the book, the soft landing that did not demand a bed, the fact that he had reached for paper instead of her — it should have settled the night. Instead it made her test the edge of it. Black smoke held low as she moved her hand and set it on his leg, deliberate enough to be a question, light enough to be taken back if the page stopped turning.
Curtis simply turned another page.
No jump. No look. No hand covering hers. No speech about destiny or the shape of the contact. Just the soft sound of paper, the shift of long black braids as he kept reading, their legs still aligned, her hand now part of the geometry he refused to turn into an event.
Celty’s inner thoughts arrived in a rush she could not type.
He did not take it.
Not rejection. Something more destabilizing. Shinra would have brightened, narrated, claimed the gesture as proof of a bond he had already decided existed. Mei would have smiled like a hypothesis confirmed. Curtis turned a page, as if a headless woman resting her hand on his leg were allowed to be only that — weight, warmth, presence — and not a door he was required to walk through.
Two nights ago I measured him in the dark.
Tonight I put my hand on him and he stayed in the book.
That should feel like being ignored.
It feels like being trusted not to need a performance.
The private channel stayed closed. She did not give him the spiral. She left her hand where it was, testing not him now but herself: how long she could sit inside a contact that was not being converted into a claim, a rescue, or a replacement for the house across the ward.
How far will I go if he keeps not stopping me?
How far will I go if he keeps not asking me to?
Another page turned.
Celty sat with her hand on his leg, the spare room empty behind her, and understood that the soft landing was more dangerous than the bed had been — because this version let her keep choosing, and so far she was still choosing closer.
Celty raised her hand higher up his leg.
Not sudden. A slow test along the line of his thigh, still over fabric, still able to be called accidental if either of them needed the lie. Black smoke stayed low. The private channel stayed shut. She was watching the book as much as the contact — waiting to see whether the next page would still turn.
It did.
Curtis’s face did not change.
The mask held: mismatched eyes on the print, braids loose, AirPods Max around his neck, the soft landing that reached for paper and let a headless woman sit close without turning her into a crisis. Another page. Steady breath. The host who would not force anything.
Under it, the turmoil had been running since the first alignment of their legs.
She’s testing.
She’s been testing since she left the spare room.
Hand. Higher. And you are sitting here like a man who cannot feel it.
He could feel it.
Every inch of the shift registered. The earlier night in his bed. The comparison she did not know he had half-caught in the dark. Anri’s doorway hug still warm in the same evening. The confession he had almost fled for a cigarette. Celty’s house erased across the ward. A month ago. And now a hand climbing his leg while he played at being only a reader, because if the mask cracked he did not trust what the soft landing would become — whatever, or a door he had already told her not to turn him into, or the other half of him that people stared at like a freak when it came out.
Turn the page.
Do not look at her.
If you look, you will stop being the version that lets her choose.
If you stop her, you decide for her.
If you lean into it, you become the thing she said she would not steal while her house is still lying.
His thumb moved. Another page.
The mask held so well it almost looked like indifference.
Inside, the boy who had said I can be your soft landing was locked in a quiet war with the boy who had said I like you a lot to someone else only hours earlier, and with the man who could hear a dullahan’s voice and still had not figured out how to hold two true things without one of them becoming a fire.
Celty’s hand stayed where she had put it.
Curtis kept reading.
Only the slight stillness in his shoulders — too controlled, too deliberate — would have given the turmoil away to anyone who knew him well enough to look for the seam in the mask.
Celty felt the page turn under her attention like a refusal that was not a refusal.
Her hand stayed higher on his leg. He did not look at her. He did not cover her fingers. He did not tell her to stop. He only kept reading, and for a second the stillness of it almost read as indifference — until she caught the seam.
The shoulders.
Too controlled. Too deliberate. A man holding a position, not a man who felt nothing. The same kind of stillness he wore when he hoped a stain was coffee and still opened the kit. The same kind of stillness that had let him say a month ago without putting a fist through the wall.
The private thought landed sharp and warm at once.
He is not calm.
He is performing calm.
Black smoke ticked once against the couch.
She had spent years beside a man who narrated every closeness into destiny. Shinra would have already named her hand, claimed it, turned it into proof. Curtis sat inside the contact and refused to seize it, and the refusal was costing him something she could see in the line of his shoulders if not in his face.
That mask is for me.
So I can still choose.
So he does not become the whatever I told him not to be.
The realization made her hand feel heavier on his thigh.
She could push farther and learn where the mask broke.
She could pull back and prove she had only been testing the distance.
She could leave the hand where it was and accept that he was giving her the rarest thing in the room: time, without a demand attached to it.
Celty did not move for a long second.
Then the private channel opened, very low, not accusing, almost quiet with something like respect.
You are not as still as you look.
She left the words there, her hand still on his leg, and watched to see whether the next page would turn — or whether the seam she had found would finally make him look at her.
Heat crept up Curtis’s neck under the loose braids.
The next page did not turn. His thumb stopped on the paper like the book had finally noticed it was being used as a shield. For a second the mask held by pure stubbornness — then the private channel opened from his side, rough and low, the seam Celty had found giving way out loud where only she could hear it.
I could only keep it up for so long, Celty.
Not a grab. Not a command. An admission. The calm reader cracked into the boy who had been in turmoil since their legs first lined up, since her hand climbed, since the soft landing stopped being theoretical and became weight on his thigh. Color touched the tops of his ears. His mismatched eyes stayed on the open book a fraction longer, then finally lifted to the empty space above her collar.
The living room felt smaller.
He did not move her hand.
He did not put his over it.
He only let her see that the stillness had been work, and that the work had a limit.
You can leave it there.
Or not.
I just can’t pretend I don’t feel it anymore.
The book stayed open on his knee, forgotten mid-sentence. Celty’s hand stayed on his leg. Between them the soft landing stopped being only principle and became a held breath, waiting on what she would do with the truth that the mask had a clock on it, and the clock had just run out.
Celty did not pull her hand back.
The admission I could only keep it up for so long landed in her like proof that the mask had been for her sake, not indifference, and that knowledge made the test harder to stop instead of easier. Black smoke rose in a slow ribbon. She shifted closer along the couch until her shoulder met his arm fully, her hand remaining higher on his thigh, no longer pretending the contact was accidental.
The private channel opened, quieter than the stain-night demand, more honest than the spare-room principle.
Then don’t keep it up.
A short pause. She was still choosing, still not climbing into his lap, still not dragging him toward the bed — but she was done pretending the hand was only curiosity.
I am not asking you to become whatever. I am not asking you to fix the desk or replace the house. I am sitting here because the spare room was a good idea that failed, and because you turned a page instead of taking something I had not finished offering.
Her fingers pressed once, deliberate, then eased without leaving him.
You said I could stay by you. You said the door stays open. This is me walking through it a little farther. If it is too much after the month, after the photos, after whatever the second cup was — tell me. If it is not…
She did not finish the sentence in the link.
She only stayed close, legs aligned, hand on his leg, the empty space above her collar turned toward his face in the low light, and let the rest of the test be whether he would meet the honesty he had just given her or put the mask back on now that she had answered it with more closeness instead of retreat.
The book remained open and unread on his knee.
The spare room remained empty.
Celty continued — not into a claim on the whole of him, but into the inch of night she could still take without turning a soft landing into a theft.
Curtis did not put the mask back on.
The private channel carried his answer low and steady, heat still in his face, hand nowhere near forcing hers higher or away.
Then proceed. I’m fine as long as you’re comfortable. I would never force you into something you’re not okay with.
The book slid a little on his knee, forgotten. Long black braids shifted as he held her gaze at the empty space above her collar, giving her the one boundary that mattered: hers. Not a dare. Not a performance of being unshakable. Permission with an exit still built into it.
Celty felt the words land and stay.
Black smoke softened. The last sharp edge of the test dulled into something more dangerous than testing — being allowed. Shinra’s love had always arrived as certainty about what she needed. This arrived as only if you want it, from a boy who had just admitted the mask had a time limit and still put her comfort ahead of the heat climbing his neck.
You keep doing that, she sent back, quieter. Handing me the choice like it is the point of the night.
She did not rush the next movement.
She settled in closer, her hand remaining where it was on his thigh, her shoulder firm against his arm, the empty line of her neck near his temple. Not a leap into the bed. Not a claim that erased the spare-room principle or the wiped desk across the ward. Just the clear, continuous choice to stay in the contact he had refused to seize.
I am comfortable, she added. That is why I am still here. If that changes, I will say so. You do the same.
The living room held them like that: a book open and unread, headphones around a boy’s neck, a headless woman’s hand on his leg, and a soft landing that had finally stopped being only an offer and become a shared rule —
proceed,
only while it is wanted,
stop the moment it is not.
Curtis let out a slow breath and did not turn the page.
Celty stayed.
The night, for once, did not demand they name what it was becoming.
Celty continued to push her limits.
Closer along the couch. More of her weight against his side. Her hand steady higher on his thigh, then a fraction higher still, mapping the line where comfort was supposed to break and finding only more of the same quiet heat. She waited for the flinch in herself — the one that said this is a theft, this is the soft landing becoming a second life, this is you using a boy who said a month ago like the wound was still open.
It did not come.
No uncomfortable arrived.
That was the part that unsettled her more than the contact.
Black smoke curled slow in the low light while Curtis stayed as he had promised: not forcing, not narrating, not turning her experiment into destiny. The private channel stayed open and mostly unused. She was too busy listening to her own absence of alarm.
Why.
Why did this not feel like the office chair.
Why did this not feel like Mei’s laugh in the kitchen.
Why did this not feel like Shinra’s certainty climbing onto her life and calling it love.
Why, when she pushed, did the night only feel like a door she was allowed to stand in instead of a room she was being locked inside.
I am closer than the spare room.
I am closer than principle.
I am touching him and he is letting me choose the inches.
And still nothing in me says stop.
The answer she did not want sat under the question.
Because he was not taking.
Because the mask had cracked into honesty instead of appetite.
Because proceed only while you’re comfortable was the opposite of every time someone else had decided what she needed for her.
Because the wiped desk across the ward made this couch feel like the first true surface she had touched all night — not clean of history, just honest about the marks it still had.
Celty’s hand stayed.
She pushed one more small limit — her forehead near his shoulder, the empty space above her collar almost resting there — and waited again for the uncomfortable to finally show up and save her from herself.
It did not.
Then the limit is not the contact, she thought, almost grim. The limit is how long I can keep calling this a test before I have to admit I want the thing I am testing.
Beside her, Curtis breathed steady, the book forgotten, the rule still in force.
Celty held the new closeness and wondered, not for the first time that night, whether the soft landing had already stopped being something he was offering…
and started being something she was taking because, for once, taking it did not feel like a crime.
The quiet broke on a question that had no business sounding so flat.
Celty’s private voice came through with the same directness she used on stains and addresses, her hand still on his leg, the closeness still unpunished by discomfort.
Have you had sex in this suite?
Curtis went flush so fast the earlier heat looked mild.
“No!”
It came out too loud for the link and the room at once, then he caught it, ears burning under the loose braids, mismatched eyes finally off the forgotten book and fixed on the empty space above her collar like she had just pulled a fire alarm in a library.
“…No,” he said again, lower, still flustered. “Nothing like that. Not in here. Not with anyone.”
The second cup in the sink suddenly felt like a witness. The doorway hug. The confession to Anri. None of which he was about to unpack on top of a dead-stop no. He left it at the fact, red in the face, the soft landing looking a lot less composed now that the question had landed in the exact place where his calm had already been failing.
Celty did not move her hand.
She only registered the flush, the speed of the answer, and the particular honesty of a boy who could face down Mei at a sushi counter and still short-circuit over a single plain question in his own living room.
I see, she sent, and if she had a mouth it would have almost smiled. That was not a trap. I wanted the true layout of the room I am sitting in.
A shorter beat, less clinical.
You can stop looking like I asked for a dissection. No is a complete sentence. I heard it.
Curtis’s flush did not cool. If anything the follow-up made it worse. He tipped his head back against the couch, long black braids shifting, and muttered it like the suite itself had joined a conspiracy.
“Why is everyone so interested in my sex life.” A rough little breath. “No. I’m not having sex with anyone right now.”
The complaint landed in the low light with Anri’s earlier hallway-hour question still ghosted under it, and Mei’s sushi-counter “functional response” questionnaire, and now Celty’s flat have you had sex in this suite. He looked cornered by his own honesty.
Celty’s hand stayed on his leg.
Her inner thoughts did not stay on his complaint.
They went sideways, two nights back, into the dark of his bed when he had already fallen asleep and she had still been measuring the differences between him and the man she went home to. The weight of him. The heat. Then the accidental brush of her hand — the moment her whole body had gone still because large was an insufficient word and holy shit had been the only private reaction that fit. She had told herself maybe one day and then called it ridiculous. She had still rested her leg over him like the thought needed pinning down before it got any louder.
Not having sex with anyone right now.
The sentence was true. She believed it. That did not stop the memory from sitting up behind her eyes: the same boy who was currently red-faced over a question had been, under those covers, an entire unspoken problem her hand had found without asking permission.
Black smoke ticked once.
I already know more about that subject than this conversation thinks I do.
I found it in the dark and pretended I was only going to sleep.
He is saying no one. He means the present tense.
The present tense is not what my hand keeps remembering.
She did not put that on the private channel.
She only looked at his flushed face, at the mouth that had just sworn he was not having sex with anyone, and felt the gap between the soft landing’s honesty and the fact that two nights ago she had already crossed a line in silence while he slept.
Everyone is interested, she thought, almost dry, because you keep answering like the truth is simple, and it never is once someone has been in your bed.
Out loud, in the link, she kept it lighter than the memory.
I was not taking a census of the city. I was asking about this room. You answered. We can leave it there.
Her hand did not leave his leg.
The memory did not leave her either.
Curtis let out a breath that was half surrender to the hour.
“I’m going to lay down.”
Celty’s answer came without a pause.
“Then I shall join you.”
She snapped her fingers. Black smoke folded around her riding gear and peeled it away in a single practiced motion, leaving her in the same night wear as two days ago: black bra, matching panties, the long pale lines of a body that did not need a head to occupy a room. She stood in the low light of his living room as if the spare-room principle had finally finished losing the argument.
Curtis’s eyes went wide, then away, then back despite himself.
“…Oh dear. I almost forgot about that.”
Celty tilted the empty space above her collar, a question in the angle.
He swallowed, flush still high under the loose braids, and the next words came out quieter than the complaint about his sex life, softer than the mask.
“You have beautiful skin. You know that.”
If Celty had a face, she would have been blushing.
As it was, the black smoke around her shoulders stuttered, then rose in a thin, uncontrolled ribbon that had nothing to do with weapons. The private channel opened on static for a second before language returned.
…You cannot just say that.
She did not cover herself. She did not turn the compliment into a joke about being a dullahan. She only stood there in black lace and bare skin, the soft landing suddenly looking a lot less like a couch arrangement and a lot more like the night she had already spent in his bed, while a boy who claimed he was not having sex with anyone looked at her like the truth of her body had reached him without needing a face to complete it.
Curtis stayed on the edge of laying down, caught between the invitation he had issued and the version of her that had just answered it.
“I mean it,” he added, rougher. “Beautiful.”
Celty’s smoke gave her away again.
She stepped toward the couch — toward him — and the later hour finished becoming the same shape it had taken two nights ago, only this time both of them were awake for the start of it.
Curtis laid down.
Celty got under the covers with him.
The couch was not a bed, but the blanket made it one. Bare shoulder against his shirt. The line of her leg along his. The same black lace he had almost forgotten, now close enough that the soft landing had a temperature. Curtis stared at the ceiling like it might offer a protocol.
His inner thoughts did not.
Ok. Stay cool. You slept beside her before. But she wasn’t touching you.
C’mon, Curtis. You’ve been touched before. Why are you being a wimp.
It was not working.
Every point of contact reported for duty at once: her hand from earlier still ghosted on his thigh, her side against his ribs, the fact that she had chosen the covers after he said proceed only if you’re comfortable. Anri’s confession was still in the same night. The wiped desk was still across the ward. A month ago was still a live wire. And he was doing mental drills under a blanket like a teenager who had never been in a room with a body before.
Justice, in the cheap seats, was no help at all.
Bahahahaha. Look at you. Dullahan in your lace, under your blanket, and you’re giving yourself a locker-room speech. You’ve been stabbed, deported, and lived in a forest, and this is what breaks the boss fight. Touching. Incredible.
Curtis’s response came out of his mouth before he could stop it, rough and too honest for the ceiling.
“…You’re really here.”
Not a question. A man verifying the weight against his side because the inner pep talk had failed and the brother’s laughter was the only other soundtrack. Long black braids spilled over the cushion. His arm stayed where it was, not around her yet, not fleeing either — stuck in the exact middle of stay cool and you’re a wimp while Celty’s beautiful skin and absent face occupied the space Justice would have paid money to watch him fail at.
Under the covers, the soft landing finished becoming physical.
Curtis, still trying to take his own advice, was losing to it in real time.
